  <p>
    Two of the diagram's labels are worth unpacking. <strong
      >Why the bar's re-render is cheap in Approach 1 but not in Approach 4:</strong
    > in Approach 1 the bar is a small server-rendered row of links that swaps in next to its sibling
    panel segment while the parent layout and the rest of the tree stay untouched, so the work is bounded
    to the one segment that changed. In Approach 4 the tab is not a segment at all, so the router has
    nothing to swap surgically: the entire page component re-executes on the server as a single unit,
    bar and panel and every param-dependent branch together. Same word on the row, a far larger blast
    radius.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The difference between a segment swapping in and a slot swapping in:</strong> a segment swap
    replaces the single active child segment beneath a persistent layout, which is ordinary nested routing
    (a layout has exactly one active child at each level, and that child is what changes). A slot swap
    navigates one named parallel-route slot (Approach 3's <code>@view</code>) while the layout's
    other children and its sibling slots keep their own state and do not re-render. That is
    precisely why Approach 3 can hold sibling state the others cannot: slots let several regions sit
    side by side and navigate independently, whereas a plain segment is the layout's one dynamic
    child.
  </p>

  <table class='tbl'>
