      <tr>
        <td><strong>1 · Segments, bar in each page</strong></td>
        <td>None</td>
        <td>Re-renders per change (cheap)</td>
        <td>Yes</td>
        <td>Yes</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>2 · Segments, bar in the layout</strong></td>
        <td>One small leaf</td>
        <td>Never repaints</td>
        <td>Yes</td>
        <td>Yes</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>3 · Parallel-route slot</strong></td>
        <td>One small leaf</td>
        <td>Bar + sibling regions never repaint</td>
        <td>Yes (slot sub-routes)</td>
        <td>Yes</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>4 · Query param</strong></td>
        <td>None required</td>
        <td>Re-renders with the page</td>
        <td>No: one module, hand-built boundaries</td>
        <td>No</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    So the full ladder reads:
    <strong
      >segments (bar in the page, or in the layout with the one-leaf indicator), a parallel slot
      when the tabs share the page with regions that must hold state, then a search param, then, for
      genuinely cosmetic switches only, state</strong
    >. Each step down should be a deliberate decision, not a habit.
    <Chip k='next' />
    <Chip k='very' />
  </p>

  <h2 id='url-writes'>9.4 Writing the URL: transitions, stale content, and optimistic tabs</h2>
  <p>
    Reading the URL is half the contract; the other half is writing it, because
    <strong>writing the URL is how a client leaf forces the server to re-render.</strong>
    <Chip k='next' />
    <Chip k='critical' /> When a leaf calls
    <code>router.replace()</code> with a new query string, the router performs a client-side transition
    to the same route: the server re-renders the page with the new
    <code>searchParams</code>, and the fresh RSC payload merges into the tree in place (<a
      href='04-ssr.html#routing'>§4.5</a
    >). Watch the network tab while it happens: no document reload, just a small RSC request
    carrying the re-rendered segments. The URL write <em>is</em> the refetch.
  </p>
  <p>
    Three refinements turn that mechanic into UI that feels instant. First,
    <strong>wrap the write in a transition</strong>: the App Router treats navigations as
    transitions, so an already-revealed Suspense boundary keeps its current content instead of
    re-showing a fallback (<a href='11-suspense-streaming.html#refetch'>§11.5</a>), and your own <code
      >useTransition</code
    > gives you <code>isPending</code> as the loading indicator. Second, <strong
      >show the stale content while the new render is in flight</strong
    >: dim it against <code>isPending</code>, or reach for
    <code>useDeferredValue</code> when the results are client-rendered; both worked examples live in <a
      href='11-suspense-streaming.html#refetch'>§11.5</a
    >. Third, and this is the one that makes tabs feel native: <strong
      >update the selection optimistically.</strong
    >
  </p>
  <p>
    The problem with URL-derived tab state alone: the active highlight comes from
    <code>searchParams</code>, which only changes when the server render lands. Click a tab and the
    highlight lags behind by the length of the render. React 19's
    <code>useOptimistic</code> closes that gap: set the optimistic value immediately inside the transition,
    and React reconciles it with the URL-derived value automatically when the new payload arrives (or
    reverts it if the navigation fails):
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">ViewTabs.tsx (inner; the baked-in Suspense wrapper of §5.6 is omitted)</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ViewTabsInner</span>() {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> searchParams = <span class="tok-fn">useSearchParams</span>();
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> router = <span class="tok-fn">useRouter</span>();
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> [isPending, startTransition] = <span class="tok-fn">useTransition</span>();

  <span class="tok-com">/* The URL is the source of truth; the optimistic value fronts for it
     while the server render is in flight. */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> urlView = searchParams.<span class="tok-fn">get</span>(<span class="tok-str">"view"</span>) ?? <span class="tok-str">"reports"</span>;
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> [view, setOptimisticView] = <span class="tok-fn">useOptimistic</span>(urlView);

  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;<span class="tok-fn">TabList</span> <span class="tok-prop">data-pending</span>={isPending}&gt;
      {TABS.map((tab) =&gt; (
        &lt;<span class="tok-fn">TabButton</span>
          <span class="tok-prop">key</span>={tab.value}
          <span class="tok-prop">active</span>={view === tab.value}
          <span class="tok-prop">onClick</span>={() =&gt;
            <span class="tok-fn">startTransition</span>(() =&gt; {
              <span class="tok-fn">setOptimisticView</span>(tab.value);
              <span class="tok-kw">const</span> params = <span class="tok-kw">new</span> <span class="tok-fn">URLSearchParams</span>(searchParams);
              params.<span class="tok-fn">set</span>(<span class="tok-str">"view"</span>, tab.value);
              router.<span class="tok-fn">replace</span>(<span class="tok-str">`?${params.toString()}`</span>);
            })
          }
        &gt;
          {tab.label}
        &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">TabButton</span>&gt;
      ))}
    &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">TabList</span>&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      The clicked tab highlights instantly: <code>setOptimisticView</code> applies before the server render
      starts, the transition carries the URL write, and when the fresh payload lands the optimistic value
      reconciles with the URL-derived one automatically. If the navigation fails, React reverts the highlight
      for free. The interaction is never blocked by the page render; only the panel content waits, dimmed,
      per
      <a href='11-suspense-streaming.html#refetch'>§11.5</a>.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>
  <p>
    The result is the best of both worlds from <a href='#tabs'>§9.3</a>'s ladder: the URL stays the
    source of truth (shareable, refresh-proof, server-rendered), and the interaction feels as
    immediate as any <code>useState</code> tab bar. The same recipe applies to any URL-backed control:
    filters, sort toggles, pagination, view switches.
  </p>

  <h2 id='filter-table'>9.5 Worked example: a filterable data table</h2>
  <p>
    Everything so far in this chapter converges on one very common screen: a page with chrome (a
    nav, a sidebar, a header), a <strong>filters bar</strong>, and a <strong>data table</strong>
    below it.
    <Chip k='next' />
    <Chip k='critical' /> The instinct from the old model is a single
    <code>"use client"</code> component that holds the filter values in <code>useState</code> and refetches
    the rows in an effect whenever they change. The server-first version inverts that: the only client
    code is the individual inputs, each writing one param to the URL, and the table is a server component
    that reads those params and fetches. Nothing else on the page is interactive, so nothing else ships
    JavaScript.
  </p>

