  <h3 id='filter-inputs'>One client leaf per input</h3>
  <p>
    Break the filters bar apart so that <strong
      >each input is its own small
      <code>"use client"</code> component.</strong
    > A status dropdown and a text search are two leaves, not one filter panel; each reads only its own
    current value (handed down from the server) and, on change, writes only its own param, in a transition,
    preserving the others. This is the push-down instinct of <a
      href='05-client-server-boundaries.html#push-down'>§5.3</a
    >
    applied to a toolbar: the boundary wraps the one control that needs a handler, not the row it sits
    in.
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">StatusFilter.tsx - one leaf, one control, one param</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">StatusFilter</span>({ value }: { value: <span class="tok-kw">string</span> }) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> router = <span class="tok-fn">useRouter</span>();
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> searchParams = <span class="tok-fn">useSearchParams</span>();
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> [isPending, startTransition] = <span class="tok-fn">useTransition</span>();

  <span class="tok-kw">function</span> <span class="tok-fn">setStatus</span>(next: <span class="tok-kw">string</span>) {
    <span class="tok-kw">const</span> params = <span class="tok-kw">new</span> <span class="tok-fn">URLSearchParams</span>(searchParams);
    next === <span class="tok-str">"all"</span> ? params.<span class="tok-fn">delete</span>(<span class="tok-str">"status"</span>) : params.<span class="tok-fn">set</span>(<span class="tok-str">"status"</span>, next);
    params.<span class="tok-fn">delete</span>(<span class="tok-str">"page"</span>); <span class="tok-com">// a new filter resets pagination</span>
    <span class="tok-fn">startTransition</span>(() =&gt; {
      router.<span class="tok-fn">replace</span>(<span class="tok-str">`?${params.toString()}`</span>, { <span class="tok-prop">scroll</span>: <span class="tok-kw">false</span> });
    });
  }

  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;select
      <span class="tok-prop">data-pending</span>={isPending ? <span class="tok-str">""</span> : <span class="tok-kw">undefined</span>}
      <span class="tok-prop">value</span>={value}
      <span class="tok-prop">onChange</span>={(e) =&gt; <span class="tok-fn">setStatus</span>(e.target.value)}
    &gt;
      &lt;option <span class="tok-prop">value</span>=<span class="tok-str">"all"</span>&gt;All statuses&lt;/option&gt;
      &lt;option <span class="tok-prop">value</span>=<span class="tok-str">"active"</span>&gt;Active&lt;/option&gt;
      &lt;option <span class="tok-prop">value</span>=<span class="tok-str">"archived"</span>&gt;Archived&lt;/option&gt;
    &lt;/select&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      The leaf reads its current value from a prop (the server passes it), and its only side effect
      is writing <code>?status</code>. <code>scroll: false</code> stops the page from jumping to the top
      on every change; deleting <code>?page</code> is the "reset the dependent state" habit, so filtering
      never strands you on a page that no longer exists. The write is the refetch (<a
        href='#url-writes'>§9.4</a
      >).
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <p>
    The search input is the same shape, with one addition: <strong>debounce the write</strong> so a round
    trip fires once the user pauses, not on every keystroke. The input stays uncontrolled (<code
      >defaultValue</code
    >) because between writes its own keystrokes are the source of truth; the URL catches up a beat
    later.
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">SearchFilter.tsx - same shape, debounced</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">SearchFilter</span>({ value }: { value: <span class="tok-kw">string</span> }) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> router = <span class="tok-fn">useRouter</span>();
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> searchParams = <span class="tok-fn">useSearchParams</span>();
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> [isPending, startTransition] = <span class="tok-fn">useTransition</span>();

  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> write = <span class="tok-fn">useDebouncedCallback</span>((q: <span class="tok-kw">string</span>) =&gt; {
    <span class="tok-kw">const</span> params = <span class="tok-kw">new</span> <span class="tok-fn">URLSearchParams</span>(searchParams);
    q ? params.<span class="tok-fn">set</span>(<span class="tok-str">"q"</span>, q) : params.<span class="tok-fn">delete</span>(<span class="tok-str">"q"</span>);
    params.<span class="tok-fn">delete</span>(<span class="tok-str">"page"</span>);
    <span class="tok-fn">startTransition</span>(() =&gt; {
      router.<span class="tok-fn">replace</span>(<span class="tok-str">`?${params.toString()}`</span>, { <span class="tok-prop">scroll</span>: <span class="tok-kw">false</span> });
    });
  }, <span class="tok-str">300</span>);

  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;input
      <span class="tok-prop">type</span>=<span class="tok-str">"search"</span>
      <span class="tok-prop">data-pending</span>={isPending ? <span class="tok-str">""</span> : <span class="tok-kw">undefined</span>}
      <span class="tok-prop">defaultValue</span>={value}
      <span class="tok-prop">onChange</span>={(e) =&gt; <span class="tok-fn">write</span>(e.target.value)}
      <span class="tok-prop">placeholder</span>=<span class="tok-str">"Search programs"</span>
    /&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      Two independent leaves, two params, and neither clobbers the other: each rebuilds the query
      from the live <code>useSearchParams()</code> and changes only its own key. Add a sort control or
      a date range later and it is one more leaf, not a rewrite of a growing filter object.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <h3 id='filter-server'>The server reads the params and refetches</h3>
  <p>
    The page is a server component. It reads the params once, hands each one to the input that
    renders it (so the controls show the current state after a reload or a shared link), and hands
    them to the table (so it fetches). The table sits behind a <a
      href='11-suspense-streaming.html#mechanism'>Suspense</a
    > boundary so its data never blocks the chrome or the filters bar from painting.
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">app/programs/page.tsx - reads params, feeds the inputs and the table</span><span class="tok-kw">export default async function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ProgramsPage</span>({ searchParams }: <span class="tok-fn">PageProps</span>) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> { status = <span class="tok-str">"all"</span>, q = <span class="tok-str">""</span> } = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> searchParams;

  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;div <span class="tok-prop">className</span>=<span class="tok-str">"group flex min-h-0 grow flex-col"</span>&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">PageHeader</span> <span class="tok-prop">title</span>=<span class="tok-str">"Programs"</span> /&gt;

      <span class="tok-com">{/* Static bar; only its inputs are client leaves. */}</span>
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">FiltersBar</span>&gt;
        &lt;<span class="tok-fn">StatusFilter</span> <span class="tok-prop">value</span>={status} /&gt;
        &lt;<span class="tok-fn">SearchFilter</span> <span class="tok-prop">value</span>={q} /&gt;
      &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">FiltersBar</span>&gt;

      <span class="tok-com">{/* Fallback shows once, on first load. A filter change keeps the old
          rows on screen and dims them (below) instead of flashing this. */}</span>
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Suspense</span> <span class="tok-prop">fallback</span>={&lt;<span class="tok-fn">TableSkeleton</span> /&gt;}&gt;
        &lt;div <span class="tok-prop">className</span>=<span class="tok-str">"transition-opacity group-has-[[data-pending]]:opacity-50"</span>&gt;
          &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ProgramsTable</span> <span class="tok-prop">status</span>={status} <span class="tok-prop">q</span>={q} /&gt;
        &lt;/div&gt;
      &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Suspense</span>&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      There is deliberately no <code>key</code> on the Suspense boundary: keying it by the params would
      remount it and re-show the skeleton on every filter change, the exact flash you are trying to avoid.
      Leaving it stable lets the transition keep the old rows visible while the new ones stream in.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">ProgramsTable.tsx - the params ARE the function signature</span><span class="tok-kw">export async function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ProgramsTable</span>({ status, q }: <span class="tok-fn">Props</span>) {
  <span class="tok-com">/* The fetch lives at the leaf that needs it (§5.3), keyed by the params.
     Changing the URL re-runs this component on the server with new args. */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> programs = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> db.programs.<span class="tok-fn">findMany</span>({
    <span class="tok-prop">where</span>: {
      <span class="tok-prop">status</span>: status === <span class="tok-str">"all"</span> ? <span class="tok-kw">undefined</span> : status,
      <span class="tok-prop">name</span>: q ? { <span class="tok-prop">contains</span>: q, <span class="tok-prop">mode</span>: <span class="tok-str">"insensitive"</span> } : <span class="tok-kw">undefined</span>,
    },
    <span class="tok-prop">orderBy</span>: { <span class="tok-prop">updatedAt</span>: <span class="tok-str">"desc"</span> },
  });

  <span class="tok-kw">if</span> (programs.length === <span class="tok-str">0</span>) <span class="tok-kw">return</span> &lt;<span class="tok-fn">EmptyState</span> <span class="tok-prop">query</span>={q} /&gt;;

  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;table&gt;
      &lt;tbody&gt;
        {programs.map((p) =&gt; (
          &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ProgramRow</span> <span class="tok-prop">key</span>={p.id} <span class="tok-prop">program</span>={p} /&gt;
        ))}
      &lt;/tbody&gt;
    &lt;/table&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      No <code>"use client"</code>, no effect, no request-state machine: the component awaits its
      data while it renders, and its arguments are the URL params. This is the whole payoff of <a
        href='#url-state'>§9.2</a
      >: the query string is the page's function signature, and the server is free to cache and
      dedupe the fetch (<a href='16-dedupe-caching.html'>§16</a>).
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <h3 id='filter-swap'>How the table swaps in without a reload</h3>
  <p>
    The cycle is the mechanic of <a href='#url-writes'>§9.4</a>, made concrete. A leaf writes the
    URL inside a transition; the router issues an RSC request for the same route; the server
    re-renders with the new <code>searchParams</code> and refetches; the fresh RSC payload for the changed
    segment merges into the tree in place (<a href='04-ssr.html#routing'>§4.5</a>). The document
    never reloads, and the parts that did not change (the chrome, the sidebar, the filters bar and
    its focus, the scroll position) are never touched.
  </p>

