  <h3 id='filter-feedback'>Feedback while it reloads: dim, do not flash</h3>
  <p>
    Because the transition holds the old rows in place, the table would otherwise sit there looking
    unchanged while the fetch runs. Give the user a signal by <strong
      >dimming the stale rows.</strong
    >
    Each leaf already flips a <code>data-pending</code> attribute on itself during its transition; a single
    <code>group-has-[[data-pending]]</code> selector on the table wrapper (in the page above) lets the
    server-rendered table dim itself while any filter is in flight, with no shared client state and no
    prop drilling. The rows fade to half opacity, then snap back to full when the new payload lands.
  </p>
  <p>
    That <code>isPending</code>-driven dim is the right tool when the results come from the server.
    When the filtering happens <em>on the client</em> instead (a large in-memory list, no round trip),
    <code>useDeferredValue</code> is the analog: it keeps the last committed list on screen while an expensive
    re-filter is prepared, and comparing the current value to the deferred one tells you the list is stale.
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">ResultsView.tsx - client-side filtering: useDeferredValue for the same dim</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ResultsView</span>({ rows, query }: { rows: Row[]; query: <span class="tok-kw">string</span> }) {
  <span class="tok-com">/* Keep the last-rendered list on screen while a new one is prepared;
     compare the two values to know the visible list is stale. */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> deferredQuery = <span class="tok-fn">useDeferredValue</span>(query);
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> isStale = query !== deferredQuery;
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> visible = <span class="tok-fn">useMemo</span>(
    () =&gt; <span class="tok-fn">filterRows</span>(rows, deferredQuery),
    [rows, deferredQuery],
  );

  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;ul <span class="tok-prop">className</span>=<span class="tok-str">"transition-opacity"</span> <span class="tok-prop">style</span>={{ <span class="tok-prop">opacity</span>: isStale ? <span class="tok-str">0.5</span> : <span class="tok-str">1</span> }}&gt;
      {visible.map((r) =&gt; (
        &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ResultRow</span> <span class="tok-prop">key</span>={r.id} <span class="tok-prop">row</span>={r} /&gt;
      ))}
    &lt;/ul&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      Same shape as the server case, different engine: <code>deferredQuery</code> lags <code
        >query</code
      >
      by one render, <code>isStale</code> is the dim flag, and the old list stays visible until the re-filter
      commits. Rule of thumb: URL write plus <code>isPending</code> for server-fetched results, <code
        >useDeferredValue</code
      > when the work is client-side (<a href='11-suspense-streaming.html#refetch'>§11.5</a>).
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <h2 id='cookies'>9.6 Cookies: preferences that follow the user, not the URL</h2>
  <p>
    Some state should survive across pages and sessions but has no business in a link: the collapsed
    sidebar, a table-density preference, the dismissed-announcement flag, theme. Put those in <strong
      >cookies</strong
    > (the other channel the request carries), and the server resolves them at first render, which is
    what kills the flash-of-wrong-state that
    <code>localStorage</code> approaches are doomed to (the server can't read
    <code>localStorage</code>, so it always renders the default first and corrects after hydration). <Chip
      k='next'
    />
    <Chip k='very' />
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">app/(dashboard)/layout.tsx</span><span class="tok-kw">import</span> { cookies } <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"next/headers"</span>;

<span class="tok-kw">export default async function</span> <span class="tok-fn">DashboardLayout</span>({ children }: <span class="tok-fn">LayoutProps</span>) {
  <span class="tok-com">/* A preference that must survive across pages and sessions, and that the
     server needs at first render to avoid a flash of the wrong layout:
     a cookie, not useState, not localStorage. */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> store = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> <span class="tok-fn">cookies</span>();
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> collapsed = store.<span class="tok-fn">get</span>(<span class="tok-str">"sidebar-collapsed"</span>)?.value === <span class="tok-str">"true"</span>;

  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Shell</span> <span class="tok-prop">sidebarCollapsed</span>={collapsed}&gt;{children}&lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Shell</span>&gt;;
}</pre>
  </Snippet>

  <p>
    The same caveat as search params applies: reading cookies makes that part of the tree
    request-time, so under PPR it belongs inside a deliberate dynamic region (<a
      href='04-ssr.html#page-cache'>§4.4</a
    >). The rule of thumb for choosing the channel:
    <strong>the URL describes the view, cookies describe the viewer.</strong> A filter is the view: anyone
    with the link should get it. A collapsed sidebar is the viewer: it would be bizarre in a shared link.
  </p>

  <h2 id='client-state'>9.7 What genuinely stays client state</h2>
  <p>
    The point is not that <code>useState</code> is bad; it's that it should hold
    <strong>interaction</strong>, not <strong>identity</strong>. Ephemeral, mid-gesture,
    this-tab-only state is exactly what it's for: the text in an input <em>before</em> it's committed
    to the URL (the debounced draft in
    <a href='11-suspense-streaming.html#refetch'>§11.5</a>), the open/closed flag of a dialog or
    menu living in its leaf (<a href='05-client-server-boundaries.html#self-contained'>§5.7</a>),
    hover and focus, an in-flight optimistic value, a drag mid-drag.
    <Chip k='react' />
    <Chip k='very' />
  </p>
  <p>
    Two questions sort every case.
    <strong>Would the user expect it back after a refresh, or want it in a shared link?</strong>
    Then it's the URL (view) or a cookie (preference).
    <strong>Does the server need it to render real content?</strong> Then it must be server-resolvable,
    full stop. Content the server can't finish is Part B's territory, entered only deliberately. If both
    answers are no, it's interaction state: keep it in the leaf, in memory, where it costs nothing.
  </p>

  <h2 id='nuqs'>9.8 Less plumbing: typed search params with nuqs</h2>
  <p>
    Everything in this chapter works with nothing but the framework:
    <code>useSearchParams</code>, <code>URLSearchParams</code>, <code>router.replace()</code>, a
    transition, and <code>useOptimistic</code> when the highlight must not lag. But the wiring in
    <a href='#url-writes'>§9.4</a> is boilerplate you will rewrite once per control, and there are packages
    that fold it into a single hook. The one worth knowing is
    <a href='https://nuqs.dev' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>nuqs</a>: type-safe search-param state
    for the App Router (among others), with a <code>useState</code>-shaped API.
    <Chip k='next' />
    <Chip k='imp' /> Setup is one provider (wrap the root layout in its <code>NuqsAdapter</code>);
    after that, a query param reads like local state:
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">SearchInput.tsx</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">import</span> { useQueryState, parseAsString } <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"nuqs"</span>;

<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">SearchInput</span>() {
  <span class="tok-com">/* Reads and writes ?q= like useState. shallow: false is the mechanic of
     §9.4: the URL write notifies the server, which re-renders the page
     with the new searchParams. */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> [query, setQuery] = <span class="tok-fn">useQueryState</span>(
    <span class="tok-str">"q"</span>,
    parseAsString.<span class="tok-fn">withDefault</span>(<span class="tok-str">""</span>).<span class="tok-fn">withOptions</span>({ <span class="tok-prop">shallow</span>: <span class="tok-kw">false</span> }),
  );

  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> &lt;<span class="tok-fn">SearchField</span> <span class="tok-prop">value</span>={query} <span class="tok-prop">onChange</span>={(v) =&gt; <span class="tok-fn">setQuery</span>(v)} /&gt;;
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      One hook replaces the manual read-parse-write pipeline: the typed parser owns defaults and
      serialization, and setting the state writes the URL. Everything stays inspectable in the
      address bar; nothing moved into component memory.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <p>
    The defaults deserve attention: nuqs updates the URL <em>shallowly</em> (client-only) unless you pass
    <code>shallow: false</code>, so opting into the server re-render stays a deliberate choice,
    exactly as this chapter frames it. Beyond that it covers the refinements of
    <a href='#url-writes'>§9.4</a> for free: a <code>startTransition</code> option surfaces
    <code>isPending</code> while the server render is in flight, a <code>history</code> option chooses
    replace-vs-push per control (<a href='#url-state'>§9.2</a>), and built-in throttling and
    debouncing tame high-frequency writers like search inputs. On the server, companion helpers (<code
      >createSearchParamsCache</code
    >, <code>createLoader</code>) parse the same param definitions inside server components, so
    client and server share one typed schema instead of two hand-rolled parsers. None of this
    changes the doctrine: the URL is still the state manager; a library like nuqs just makes the
    right pattern the ergonomic one.
  </p>

  <h2 id='rules'>The rules</h2>
  <table class='tbl'>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Rule</th>
        <th>Scope</th>
        <th>Importance</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>
          State the first render depends on <strong>must be server-resolvable</strong>: the URL,
          route params, search params, or cookies; never component memory.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='next' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='critical' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Filters, search, sort, pagination, tabs, view modes, date ranges:
          <strong>the view lives in the URL</strong>.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='next' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='very' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <strong>Tabs are routes</strong>: segments first, a search param second,
          <code>useState</code> only for cosmetic switches, deliberately.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='next' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='very' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Cross-page, cross-session preferences go in
          <strong>cookies, read on the server</strong>: the URL describes the view; cookies describe
          the viewer.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='next' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='very' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>useState</code> holds <strong>interaction, not identity</strong>: drafts mid-edit,
          open leaf widgets, hover/focus, in-flight optimism.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='react' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='very' /></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

